WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily, episode 45.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.480
I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.720
Today is July 26th, 2026.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.260
Here's what's happening at SpaceX today.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.620
Starship Flight 13's intact upper-stage splashdown

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.660
moves the program one step closer

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:20.480
to attempting the first tower catch of the ship on Flight 14.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:24.520
SpaceX announced plans to attempt catching the ship

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.500
upper-stage with the launch tower on Flight 14,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.140
provided post-flight data review shows no issues.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.660
The target follows Flight 13's successful upper-stage splashdown

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:36.820
in the Indian Ocean.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.520
This would mark the first try at recovering the upper-stage

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.200
via tower arms rather than ocean recovery.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.300
The decision hinges on heat shield performance

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.380
and control data collected yesterday.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.400
Elon Musk stated the attempt will proceed

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:54.360
unless mission data review uncovers problems.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.660
Remember, we covered Flight 13 yesterday.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.960
Today's announcement moves the reuse goal forward

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.640
by confirming the catch attempt.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.760
Flight 13 provided new data on heat shield tile performance

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.180
during reentry heating.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.480
Engineers observed how the updated tile layout

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.260
and attachment methods held up

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.720
through peak thermal loads on the upper-stage.

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.440
The test highlighted specific zones

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.820
where tile gaps or attachment stresses

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.020
remain concerns for repeated flights.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:26.400
Data review will guide any tile redesign

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:29.340
or application changes ahead of Flight 14.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.800
The observations focused on how the tiles behaved

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.120
under the exact heating profile experienced by the ship.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.440
From an engineering standpoint,

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.180
the planned tower catch of the ship upper-stage

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.100
on Flight 14 rests on a narrow window

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.020
of timing, velocity, and structural margin.

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:49.480
The ship must arrive at the tower

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:52.440
with residual propellant low enough to limit mass

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.860
yet high enough for final steering burns.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.260
While the tower arms must absorb

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:58.820
the remaining kinetic energy

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.080
without exceeding the stack's bending limits.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.780
The energy to be dissipated scales

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.140
with the square of the final approach speed.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.900
Even a few meters per second difference

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.300
changes the force the arms must handle by tens of percent.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.560
Reusing the tower structure

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.400
instead of building new ocean recovery hardware

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:20.080
collapses the per flight cost of the ship.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.140
From the price of a new vehicle

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.560
down toward the marginal cost of propellant and inspection.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.920
Heat shield tile performance directly governs

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.780
how much margin remains for that final guidance phase.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.580
Each tile must survive peak heating

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:36.700
while maintaining attachment integrity

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.440
so that no single gap grows into a larger thermal breach

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.220
that alters the vehicle's lift to drag ratio.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.200
The Flight 13 test exposed attachment zones

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.560
where differential expansion between tile

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.680
and substrate created measurable stress concentrations.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.420
If those stresses can be reduced through revised bonding

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.860
or edge sealing, the ship can carry the same tile mass

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.540
on subsequent flights without added reinforcement.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.640
That reduction in mass directly improves

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.760
the propellant margin available for the tower catch burns,

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.180
tightening the overall cost curve.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.820
The tower catch architecture itself

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:17.100
represents a deliberate trade between ground infrastructure

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:20.400
investment and per flight operations cost.

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.020
Building and maintaining the mechanical arms

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.480
and their load paths adds fixed capital

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.080
yet it removes the need for ocean going recovery assets

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.180
and the associated logistics chain.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:34.960
In raw input terms, the dominant expense shifts

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:38.100
from fabricating an entire new ship to the electricity,

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.900
propellant and labor required for inspection

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.020
and refurbishment.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.680
Closing that gap requires the ship

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:46.860
to survive reentry with enough precision

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.180
that the final descent burns stay within the tower's capture

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:50.720
and below.

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:54.160
Flight 14 will supply the first real data

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.320
on whether the current control and thermal protection stack

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.420
can deliver that precision at scale.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.200
The Flight 13 mission carried the latest Starlink V3 satellites

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:08.040
and achieved upper-stage recovery via Indian ocean splashdown.

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.220
The satellites represent the current generation

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.820
of higher capacity hardware.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.660
The launch demonstrated continued progress

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.040
on both the Starship vehicle and the Starlink constellation

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:19.720
refresh.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.820
Payload deployment occurred as planned

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.100
before the controlled reentry sequence.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.680
Engineers will examine telemetry on thermal protection

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.940
and guidance accuracy before finalizing the Flight 14 profile.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:36.360
The announcement came hours after the upper stage

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.340
completed its soft splashdown in the Indian ocean.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.560
Source reporting from satnews.com and Tesla Roddy

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.120
confirms the conditional tower catch target.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.140
A Falcon 9 lifted off from Vandenberg Space Force Base

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:52.580
carrying a batch of Starlink satellites.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.520
The mission used a previously flown booster

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:57.860
and followed standard West Coast trajectory.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.940
This launch adds to the ongoing cadence

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:04.060
of Falcon 9 flights supporting constellation growth.

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.400
The booster performed its return to launch site

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:11.100
or drone ship landing as planned after payload deployment.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:14.500
Observers on X noted the visible plume

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:17.520
from the Vandenberg Falcon 9 launch reaching parts

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.540
of Arizona under clear conditions.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:22.960
The sighting occurred during the booster's ascent phase

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.760
and was captured in multiple time-lapse videos

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.300
shared across the platform.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.400
West Coast launch watchers shared photos

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.660
of the booster landing on the drone ship

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:34.280
after the Starlink deployment.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:37.340
The images showed the booster's grid fins

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:40.820
and landing legs in the expected post-flight configuration.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.020
Source coverage from Fox News documented

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:45.980
the standard recovery sequence.

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.900
Cursor launched router, a request level classifier

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.580
that routes coding tasks between high performance

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.520
and lower cost models to reduce inference spend

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.360
while maintaining output quality.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.700
The system evaluates each prompt at intake

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:02.520
and selects the appropriate model

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.540
without requiring user intervention.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.240
Early benchmarks shared by the company

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:10.440
showed comparable results on standard coding suites

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.560
at lower average cost per request.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.760
Users highlighted the cursor router release

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.580
as a practical way to lower inference costs

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.160
for coding workflows without quality loss.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:24.500
Early adopters reported measurable reductions

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:27.540
in token spend on mixed difficulty tasks.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.480
The release focuses on prompt evaluation at intake

00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:33.400
to choose models automatically.

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:37.360
Engineers discussed the heat shield tile attachment changes

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.260
observed on flight 13 and what they imply

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:41.800
for tile reuse margins.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.140
Several threads compared the new attachment method

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.080
to earlier flights and speculated

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.520
on required adjustments for repeated use.

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:52.440
Community members tracked the exact timing

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.140
of the upper stage splash down

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:56.980
and compared it to prior flight 12 data points.

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.660
Timestamps from official updates were cross-referenced

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.500
with independent tracking stations

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.200
to refine reentry models.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:08.400
Local and bipartisan resistance to new AI data centers

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.940
is growing over noise, water consumption,

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:12.360
and electricity demand.

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.440
Communities in multiple states are pushing back

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.700
on proposed sites even when operators

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:19.780
offer voluntary cost protections.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:23.640
Resolving the tension will require either citing changes

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.920
or enforceable grid impact agreements

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.180
that satisfy residents and regulators.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:31.560
Several county-level hearings have already delayed

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:32.660
project timelines.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.220
The pattern reflects growing pushback

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:37.200
as AI training cluster scale.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.060
Several proposed sites have already faced

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:42.180
formal complaints from community groups.

00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:44.600
Source material from Boeing, Boeing,

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.760
and the register notes the cross-party local opposition.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.640
On the AI front, Elon Musk said Grok could generate

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:55.160
a full historical film on the scale of the Odyssey movie

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:58.180
pointing to progress in long context video

00:07:58.180 --> 00:07:59.620
and narrative generation.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:03.980
The claim centers on advances in long form video generation

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.540
and narrative coherence.

00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:08.420
No timeline or training details were provided

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.000
for reaching that output level.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:12.580
The statement highlights ongoing work

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.400
on extended context handling within the model.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.100
The comment came during a public discussion

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.580
of model capabilities and did not include

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.400
specific parameter counts or training schedules.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:27.480
It underscores the focus on extending coherent output length

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:29.140
in current development cycles.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.360
Source reporting from meme-burn captured the remarks

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:33.780
on model capabilities.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:38.360
Trump's expanded voluntary pledge asks data center operators

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:41.080
to shield households from rising power costs

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.420
tied to AI infrastructure build out.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.060
The expanded pledge asks data center operators

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:50.500
to absorb power cost increases rather than pass them

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:51.780
to residential customers.

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:55.140
The voluntary framework targets regions

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.460
with rapid AI infrastructure growth.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.600
It does not impose new mandates,

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:03.160
but signals continued political attention to grid impacts.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.840
Operators in multiple states have already signaled support

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:07.680
for the approach.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:10.180
The framework remains non-binding

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.660
and relies on operator commitments rather

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.280
than regulatory enforcement.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.960
Industry groups have begun outlining

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.640
internal cost allocation plans in response.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.880
Source coverage from the register outlines

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:24.760
the non-mandatory structure.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.940
Before we go, flight 14 heat shield and control data

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.320
will decide whether the tower catch profile gets locked in.

